<p>Andrew E. Thorberg, born in Ronberg, Norway on 20 May 1857, to a family which included two brothers and three sisters. The family immigrated to America, but Andrew came alone to Davenport, Iowa in 1876. He worked and attended school for a year to become acquainted with the English language. In 1878 he struck out with the West Coast in mind, but he came to Dakota Territory with the survey crew working for the Northern Pacific Railroad platting the new town of Mandan. The Missouri Slope region satisfied his desires, and he remained here once the survey crew finished its work. Initially he made his living as a trapper during which he became well acquainted with the countryside and its resources. Thorberg's outdoor life gave him ample opportunities to observe the soil and natural resources of the region and formed the foundation for his life values. A.E. Thorberg c. 1883 (Will P. Martin Portrait)</p>

